<!-- Add this section to tableam.sgml after the introduction -->

 <sect1 id="tableam-implementations">
  <title>Built-in Table Access Methods</title>

  <para>
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> comes with the following table access
   methods:
  </para>

  <sect2 id="tableam-heap">
   <title>heap</title>

   <para>
    The <literal>heap</literal> access method is the default storage engine.
    It implements traditional row-oriented storage with multi-version
    concurrency control (MVCC) based on transaction IDs. Key characteristics:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Creates new tuple versions for updates (copy-on-write)
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Uses TOAST for large attributes
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Requires periodic VACUUM to reclaim dead tuples
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Subject to transaction ID wraparound
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="tableam-recno">
   <title>recno</title>

   <para>
    The <literal>recno</literal> access method provides an alternative storage
    engine optimized for update-heavy OLTP workloads. It uses time-based MVCC
    with in-place updates to reduce storage bloat. See <xref linkend="recno"/>
    for comprehensive documentation.
   </para>

   <para>
    Key innovations in RECNO:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>In-place updates</emphasis> - Modifies tuples directly when
      possible, avoiding version proliferation
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Time-based MVCC</emphasis> - Uses 64-bit timestamps instead
      of transaction IDs, eliminating wraparound issues
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Integrated overflow pages</emphasis> - Replaces TOAST with
      more efficient large attribute storage
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Built-in compression</emphasis> - Automatic attribute-level
      compression with multiple algorithms
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Proactive space management</emphasis> - Opportunistic
      defragmentation reduces VACUUM frequency
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    RECNO implements the complete <structname>TableAmRoutine</structname>
    interface and supports all PostgreSQL features including indexes,
    constraints, triggers, and replication.
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="tableam-example">
  <title>Example: Implementing a Custom Table Access Method</title>

  <para>
   The RECNO implementation in <filename>src/backend/access/recno/</filename>
   provides a complete example of a custom table access method. Key files to
   study:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <filename>recno_handler.c</filename> - Main handler function and
     TableAmRoutine callbacks
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <filename>recno_tuple.c</filename> - Tuple formation and access
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <filename>recno_operations.c</filename> - Insert, update, delete
     implementations
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <filename>recno_mvcc.c</filename> - Time-based MVCC visibility
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <filename>recno_xlog.c</filename> - WAL integration
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   When implementing your own table access method, RECNO demonstrates:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     How to handle all required callbacks in TableAmRoutine
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Integration with PostgreSQL's WAL system
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Custom page and tuple formats
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Alternative MVCC implementations
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Space management and free space tracking
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>
 </sect1>